PyBEST 2.2.0: three measured performance fixes in the GPU offload path

Apply from the top of an unpacked pybest.v2.2.0 tree:

    patch -p1 < pybest-2.2.0-perf.patch

All three change performance only. Energies are unchanged to every printed
digit, and the two numpy rewrites were checked bitwise against the originals at
every shape and offset tried. Measured on an NVIDIA GB200 (RIKYU, RIKEN R-CCS)
with RCCSD on (H2O)10, Cholesky threshold 1e-5, frozen core.

  1. linalg/_gpu_support.py
     Device-to-host transfers for the PyTorch backend go through one reused
     pinned buffer per dtype instead of a fresh pageable array per transfer.
     cudaMemcpyAsync cannot use the DMA engine for a pageable destination, so
     the driver stages it through a bounce buffer with a synchronous CPU memcpy.
     65.8 GB of D2H traffic at 240 AO: 3.22 GB/s before, 192.7 GB/s after.
     CuPy is deliberately excluded -- it already stages through a pinned pool
     and reaches 126 GB/s unaided, and interposing on it is a regression.

  2. linalg/crosslib_batching.py
     Seven call sites consume the transferred array inside the expression that
     receives it (`destination[...] += ...`), so they can take a non-owning view
     of the staging buffer rather than a copy. Attributing every transfer by
     caller line, those sites carry 96-100% of all D2H bytes; the four sites
     that return the array keep the owning form.

  3. linalg/dense/dense_four_index.py
     assign_triu built np.triu_indices(nacto*nactv) on every call -- 9.02 GiB of
     int64 index arrays plus a 605-million-element scatter at 920 AO -- for a
     triangle whose rows are contiguous slices. 7.1x.
     iadd_transpose materialised two full temporaries; for an (o,v,o,v) array
     the permutation (2,3,0,1) is exactly matrix transpose on the (ov,ov) view,
     so it is M += M.T and blocks in place. 5.0x. Together, RCCSD: unravel at
     920 AO falls from 461.2 s to 61.9 s.

Effect on a complete RCCSD. The first two rows are this patch bind-mounted over
an installed PyBEST 2.2.0, stock against patched inside one job (164390):

    240 AO   100.35 s -> 45.28 s    -54.9%
    580 AO   853.06 s -> 571.13 s   -33.0%
    920 AO                          -16%     (product of in-job ratios)

The 580 AO figure agrees with the monkey-patched prototype to 0.4% (571.13 s
against 568.70 s), and energies are unchanged to every printed digit.

MEMORY: the pinned staging buffer is one allocation per dtype, sized to the
largest single transfer, and it is NOT free. Peak resident memory at 580 AO
rises 44.81 -> 47.73 GiB, +2.9 GiB; at 240 AO the change is +0.007 GiB. Expect
roughly +9 GiB at 920 AO, where the largest device-to-host transfer is 9.7 GB.
The other two changes cost nothing, and the view form reduces peak slightly by
not allocating an array per transfer.

Not included: PyBEST calls clean_memory() from ~40 sites in crosslib_batching,
and at 920 AO torch.cuda.empty_cache() costs 1059.8 s over 2399 calls against
1002.9 s of GPU kernel time. The flush exists because memory_usage() reports
driver-level free VRAM and excludes the caching allocator's cached-but-free
blocks. Correcting that accounting looks like the larger win, but it is still
under test and is not part of this patch.

